## 8. MCP surface

| Tool | From | Input |
|---|---|---|
| `get_scene` | now | — returns `view`, `selection` and all nodes (groups from 03.7, with each node's `parent`) |
| `draw_boxes` | 03.1 | `{ boxes: [{ kind, x, z, width, depth, height?, y?, rotation?, color?, name?, parent? }] }` (`parent` from 03.7) |
| `update_nodes` | 03.1 (replaces `update_boxes`) | `{ changes: [{ id, ...any of x, y, z, width, depth, height, rotation, color, kind, name, parent }] }`. `parent` from 03.7; groups accept only `name` and `parent`. |
| `remove_nodes` | 03.1 | `{ ids }`. Removing a group removes its descendants. |
| `move_nodes` | 03.7 | `{ ids, dx?, dy?, dz? }`: a relative move of boxes and whole groups |
| `rotate_nodes` | 03.7 | `{ ids, degrees }`: around the center of the given nodes' combined bounds |
| `group_nodes` | 03.7 | `{ ids, name? }` → the new group's ID |
| `ungroup` | 03.7 | `{ ids }` |

- **Relative moves are the agent's main tool for groups.** "Move the lobby 6 m east" is one `move_nodes` call, not a rewrite of every child's absolute coordinates, which is exactly where an LLM would slip.
- The agent resolves names ("the lobby") to IDs from `get_scene`. Tools only take IDs, so there's never any ambiguity.
- Every call is one undo step. All-or-nothing validation, with errors that name the failing item, as today.
- The conventions text in the tool descriptions is rewritten for: the center-based footprint, `y`, the rotation direction, the palette keys, names, groups and the selection. **After 03.1 the agent's old mental model (min corner, `room_N`) is wrong:** restart the server and reconnect with `/mcp`.

